```jsx
export const CreateObjectView = observer(({ model }) => {
  const assetRef = useRef();

  const handleCreateObject = () => {
    const value = assetRef.current.value;
    model.createObject(value).then(() => {
      // Handle success
    });
    model.setSelectedAsset(value);
  };

  const handleCleanupScene = () => {
    model.cleanupScene().then(() => {
      // Handle success
    });
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <label htmlFor="asset-select">Asset:</label>
      <input type="text" ref={assetRef} />
      <button onClick={handleCreateObject}>Create Object</button>

      <h2>Available Assets:</h2>
      <ul id="asset-list">
        {model.availableAssets.map(asset => (
          <li key={asset.id}>
            {asset.name}
            <button id={`delete-${asset.id}`}>Delete</button>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      <button id="cleanup-scene" onClick={handleCleanupScene}>Cleanup Scene</button>
    </div>
  );
});
```

In this example, I've added `ref` to the `Asset` input field and assigned it to a `useRef` hook called `assetRef`. This will allow us to access the input value when we click the "Create Object" button.

I've also added `id` attributes to the buttons so that they can be accessed via JavaScript later on. The `CreateObjectView` component now returns a div with various HTML elements, including a label, input field, buttons, and a list of available assets. The list items have unique `id` attributes based on their corresponding asset IDs.

The `observer` decorator is still used to implement React's context for updating the view automatically as the model state changes.

