Here's the JavaScript function that implements this logic:

```javascript
function determineMostRelevantProtocol(inputData) {
  let relevantProtocol = null;

  // Check for IPv6
  if (inputData.sourceIP && inputData.sourceIP.includes("::") && inputData.destinationIP && inputData.destinationIP.includes("::")) {
    relevantProtocol = "IPv6";
  }

  // Check for HTTP
  const httpPort = 80;
  if (relevantProtocol === "IPv6" && inputData.destinationPort === httpPort) {
    relevantProtocol += " with HTTP";
  }

  // Return most relevant protocol
  return relevantProtocol || "Unknown Protocol";
}

// Example usage
const inputData = {
  "sourceIP": "2001:0db8:85a3:0000:0000:8a2e:0370:7334",
  "destinationIP": "2001:0db8:85a3:0000:0000:8a2e:0370:7335",
  "port": 80,
  "payload": "Hello World"
};

console.log(determineMostRelevantProtocol(inputData));
```

This function returns "IPv6 with HTTP" if both source and destination IP addresses are in IPv6 format and the destination port is equal to the HTTP port. Otherwise, it falls back to returning "Unknown Protocol". You can adjust the function's implementation to suit your specific use case and desired protocols.

